Formula <a href="#eq:source-1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:source-1">[eq:source-1]</a> defines a finite random variable, and $$D_n(b_n):=n^{1/3}-\sum_{a=1}^n l_{a,n}
       \ \Longrightarrow\ D(b)$$ jointly with the edge coordinates. Almost surely $\sum_a l_a=\infty$. The law of $(Z_a)_{a\ge1}$ conditioned on $S=D(b)$ has a version $\Pi=\Pi_g$ characterized by continuous conditional densities. It is independent of the admissible choice of $b$.*


*Let $\mu^{\rm sp}$ denote normalized surface measure on the sphere of radius $\sqrt n$, tilted by $\exp(X^{\mathsf T}WX/2)$. The fixed-coordinate marginals of $n^{-1/3}U^{\mathsf T}X$ under $\mu^{\rm sp}$ converge jointly in distribution with the eigenvalues to the random marginals of $\Pi$. On the spectral representations of Remark <a href="#rem:edge-tight-convergence" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="rem:edge-tight-convergence">3</a>, this is ordinary weak convergence of measures, including convergence of every fixed polynomial moment. On the sets in Remark <a href="#rem:edge-tight-convergence" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="rem:edge-tight-convergence">3</a>, for every fixed $0\le j<\infty$ one has $$\label{eq:edge-coordinate-moments}
 \int |x_a|^j\,d\Pi\le C_j l_a^{j/2},
 \qquad
 \mu^{\rm sp}|x_a|^j\le C_j l_{a,n}^{j/2},$$ uniformly in the retained coordinate $a$.*


*For any sufficiently large fixed $m$, the tail $(x_a)_{a>m}$ under $\Pi$ has a density relative to $\bigotimes_{a>m}N(0,l_a)$. Conditional on this tail, the first $m$ coordinates lie on the sphere of squared radius $$\label{eq:edge-polar-radius}
 D(b)+\sum_{a\le m}l_a-\sum_{a>m}(x_a^2-l_a),$$ with the Gaussian angular density on that sphere. In particular, $\sum_a(x_a^2-l_a)=D(b)$ holds $\Pi$-almost surely.

There are $a,\xi>0$ and a sufficiently small fixed upper scale $p_{\mathrm{top}}>0$ such that $$\label{eq:source-7}
 |\alpha_p(h_p)|\le C k_p^{-a}p^2
 \qquad(r\le p\le p_{\mathrm{top}})$$ outside a set of $\mathbf P$-probability at most $C\exp(-n^\xi)$. On disorder events of probability arbitrarily close to one, the same whole-path statement holds under $Q_\mu$, with a possibly smaller $\xi$.

Fix a bounded range for $\|h\|/(p^2M_p)$ and take the upper scale sufficiently small. Uniformly for fields in this range, $$\label{eq:source-9}
 E_U L_p(h)^2\le \exp(C+Cnp^4).$$ For a sufficiently large fixed $C_1$, the contribution to this normalized second moment of cube pairs satisfying $|n^{-1}X\cdot X'|>C_1p$ is at most $C\exp(-a k_p)$. At $p=r$, the stronger estimates $$\label{eq:source-10}
 \begin{split}
 E_U(L_r-1)^2&\le Cn^{-1/4},\\
 E_U\!\left[L_r^2\|m_r-m_r^{\mathrm{sp}}\|^2\right]
 &\le C M_r^2 n^{-1/4}
 \end{split}$$ hold for the full mean vectors, written in spectral coordinates. The first bound also holds for the uncapped zero-field likelihood $L_\circ$. The pair-tail assertion at scale $r$ also holds in that uncapped case.

For every fixed $j<\infty$, after reducing the fixed upper scale if needed, $$\label{eq:source-11}
 \mathbf E\bigl(\|m_p\|/M_p\bigr)^j
 +\mathbf E\bigl(\|m_p^{\mathrm{sp}}\|/M_p\bigr)^j\le C_j.$$ At the smallest scale, $$\label{eq:source-12}
 \mathbf E\frac{\|m_r-m_r^{\mathrm{sp}}\|^2}{M_r^2}
 \le Cn^{-1/20},\qquad
 \mathbf E\|\nabla_{u_r}\log L_r\|^4=o(1).$$

There is a fixed $\tau>0$ such that, on each scale interval of a fixed bounded ratio about $q$, $$\label{eq:source-13}
 \int\|a_p^{\mathrm{err}}\|^2\,d(p^2)
 \le Ck_q(q^\tau+k_q^{-\tau})$$ outside a set of $\mathbf P$-probability at most $C\exp(-n^\xi)$. The bound holds simultaneously on a geometric list of intervals, with fixed enlargements, and also holds in expectation.

Put $D_p^K=p^2K_p-\mathop{\mathrm{Id}}$ on $\mathcal H_p$. After decreasing $\tau>0$ if necessary, $$\label{eq:source-14}
 \int_q^{2q}\frac{\|D_p^K\|_{\mathrm F}^2}{k_p}\,d\log p
 \le C(q^\tau+k_q^{-\tau})$$ outside an event whose intersection with $\mathcal G_n$ has $\mathbf P$-probability at most $C\exp(-n^\xi)$. Fixed enlargements of the interval are allowed, and the assertions hold simultaneously on a geometric list.

For a cap field $h$, write $\widetilde h=U^{\mathsf T}h$ and $\widetilde\phi_p(\widetilde h)=\phi_p(U\widetilde h)$. Let $A$ be a fixed skew-symmetric matrix supported on finitely many leading spectral coordinates, and set $\mathcal R=(A\widetilde h)\cdot\nabla_{\widetilde h}$. There is $\tau'>0$ such that $$\label{eq:source-15}
 \int_q^{2q}
 \frac{\mathbf E|\mathcal R\widetilde\phi_p(\widetilde h_p)|^2}
      {n^{2/3}p^2}\,d\log p
 \le C_A(q^{\tau'}+k_q^{-\tau'}).$$

There is a fixed $p_*>0$ with the following property. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there are finite constants $C$, $\xi>0$ and disorder events $\mathcal V_n$ with $\liminf_n\mathbb P(\mathcal V_n)\ge1-\varepsilon$ such that, on $\mathcal V_n$, simultaneously for every real function $f$ on the cube and every $r\le p\le p_*$, $$\label{eq:source-16}
 V_p(f):=Q_\mu[\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}(f\mid h_p)]
 \le Cp^{-2}\mathcal E(f)+Ce^{-n^\xi}\|f\|_\infty^2.$$ The constants and the disorder event are independent of $f$. The inequality also holds componentwise and may be summed for vector-valued functions.

On the coarse annulus, let $D=p^2K_p-\mathop{\mathrm{Id}}$, $e=h_p/\|h_p\|$, $z=D_{\perp e}$, and define, on $e^\perp$, $$E_p=(D_{\perp\perp}+zz^{\mathsf T}/|D_{ee}|)_+,$$ extended by zero on $e$. Put $s_p^+=\|D_+\|$. Then $D\preceq E_p$ and $$\label{eq:source-19}
 \|E_p\|\le Cs_p^+,
 \qquad
 \frac{\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}E_p}{k_p}
 \le C\frac{\|D\|_{\mathrm F}}{\sqrt{k_p}}+\frac C{k_p}.$$

For $x=c/p\in[\gamma,2\gamma]$, $$\label{eq:source-21}
 \|P_cE_pP_c\|
 \le C\left(\frac{\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}E_p}{k_p}+x^3\|E_p\|\right)$$ outside a set of $\mathbf P$-probability at most $C\exp(-a_\gamma k_p)$. This is an unconditional assertion with respect to the unused spectral refinement; its exceptional set may afterwards be intersected with $\mathcal G_n$.

For $r\le p\le p_*$ in the range of Proposition <a href="#prop:variance-main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:variance-main">16</a>, $$Q_\mu\|\nabla_{u_p}F_p\|^2
       \le C T\mathcal E(f)+o(1),
 \label{eq:source-26}$$ and $$\begin{split}
 \|n^{1/3}(f-S_pf)\|_{L^2(\mu)}
       &\le Cp^{-1}\sqrt{T\mathcal E(f)}+o(1),\\
 \left\|n^{1/3}(D_iS_pf)_i-J_p^f\right\|_{L^2(\mu;\mathbb R^n)}
       &\le Cp\sqrt{T\mathcal E(f)}+o(1).
 \end{split}
 \label{eq:source-27}$$ In particular, $J_p^f$ is bounded in $L^2(\mu;\mathbb R^n)$.

Suppose $f_n$ satisfies the preceding bounds and has weak cylinder limit $F$. Choose a subsequence on which $T\mathcal E(f_n)$ tends to its liminf. After further extraction in the quenched convention, there is a sequence $q_j\downarrow0$ such that the spectral components of $\overline{J^f}_{q_j}$ have weak cylinder limits $G^{(j)}$ as $n\to\infty$. Every weak cluster point of $(G^{(j)})$ in $L^2(\Pi;\ell^2)$ equals $\nabla F$. In particular, $F$ belongs to the form domain of Proposition <a href="#prop:gradient-domain" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:gradient-domain">22</a> and $$\|\nabla F\|_{L^2(\Pi;\ell^2)}
 \le C\left(\liminf_{n\to\infty}T\mathcal E(f_n)\right)^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:source-28}$$ The constant is the same for all sequences with the stated size bounds.

Fix $K<\infty$. Suppose that $W$ is symmetric, has zero diagonal, and obeys $$\|W\|\le K,\qquad \epsilon_n:=\max_{i,j}|W_{ij}|\le n^{-49/100}.$$ There is $C=C(K)\ge1$ such that, for each fixed integer $m\ge1$, uniformly in these matrices and in real functions $f$ on $\{-1,1\}^n$, $$N_m(Hf)\le\sum_{l=1}^{m+2}
 \bigl(C^{m-l+3}(m+1)^{m-l+2}+o_{n;m}(1)\bigr)N_l(f).
 \label{eq:source-35}$$ Here $o_{n;m}(1)$ is deterministic, nonnegative, and tends to zero as $n\to\infty$ with $m$ fixed.

For almost every edge configuration $g$, the series defining $B_g(z)$ is finite for every $z>0$. For every finite collection $z_1,\ldots,z_k>0$, $$(B_n(z_1),\ldots,B_n(z_k))
 \ \Longrightarrow\ (B_g(z_1),\ldots,B_g(z_k)).$$ Moreover, for each $z,\epsilon>0$, $$\lim_{\ell\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \mathbb P\left\{
 \sum_{a>\ell}\langle x_a,(z+TH)^{-1}x_a\rangle_\mu
 >\epsilon\right\}=0.$$

For every $0<\epsilon\le1$ there is $a_\epsilon>0$ such that $$\label{eq:comparison-overlap-tail}
 \sup_{0\le t\le1}\mathbb P_t\left\{
   \mu^{\otimes2}(|\rho|\ge\epsilon)>e^{-a_\epsilon n}
 \right\}\longrightarrow0.$$ Consequently, for fixed $C,K<\infty$, uniformly over nonnegative, possibly disorder-dependent functions $p$ satisfying $\mu p\le1$ and $\|p\|_\infty\le Cn^K$, the quantities $$\frac1{\binom n2}\sum_{i<j}\big|\mu[pX_iX_j]\big|$$ converge to zero in probability, uniformly in $t$. They also converge to zero in expectation, uniformly in $t$.

Uniformly over the disorder and the pair $e$, $$\label{eq:source-44}
 R_e^2\le\frac{2T}{z}\Gamma_e.$$ For $S=S^{[2]}$ one also has $R_e^2\le C_z$, whereas for $S=S^{[1]}$, $$\label{eq:source-45}
 R_e^2\le C_z\bigl(1+n^{1/3}\sqrt{B_n^{[2]}(z)}\bigr).$$ Consequently $\operatorname{avg}_eR_e^2\le C_z n^{-1/3}$. Its fourth-power analogue is $\operatorname{avg}_eR_e^4\le C_z n^{-1/3}$ for $S^{[2]}$, and $$\label{eq:source-46}
 \operatorname{avg}_eR_e^4
 \le C_z\bigl(n^{-1/3}+\sqrt{B_n^{[2]}(z)}\bigr)
 \qquad\text{for }S^{[1]}.$$

For either feature, $\operatorname{avg}_e(h_e+h_e^4)\le C_z$ deterministically. There is a deterministic sequence $\epsilon_n\to0$ such that, uniformly $t\in[0,1]$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_t\operatorname{avg}_e(h_e+h_e^4)&\le\epsilon_n
       &&\text{for }S^{[2]},\label{eq:comparison-average-second}\\
 \mathbb E_t\operatorname{avg}_e(h_e+h_e^4)&\le\epsilon_n+
             C_z\mathbb E_t\sqrt{B_n^{[2]}(z)}
       &&\text{for }S^{[1]}.
       \label{eq:comparison-average-first}
\end{aligned}$$

Fix finitely many traces $B_a=B_n^{[k_a]}(z_a)$, with $k_a\in\{1,2\}$ and $z_a>0$, and a smooth function $F$ on a neighborhood of $[0,1]^m$. For an arbitrary base disorder and pair $e$, let $B_a(\theta)$ denote the traces after adding $\theta$ to the interaction coefficient of $e$, and let $\mathfrak h_e=\sum_{a=1}^m h_{e,a}$, with each sensitivity computed at the base disorder. Then for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{eq:comparison-fourth-derivative}
 \sup_{|\theta|\le2\log n/\sqrt n}
 \left|\frac{d^4}{d\theta^4}
 F\left(\frac1{1+B_1(\theta)},\ldots,
        \frac1{1+B_m(\theta)}\right)\right|
 \le C_F(\mathfrak h_e+\mathfrak h_e^4).$$ At every real base disorder, derivatives of orders at most four also have a deterministic bound polynomial in $n$.
